{"subjectId":"java","id":"operators","title":"Operators & Precedence","group":"Getting Started","order":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Java's operators are C-like, with a few Java-specific twists: <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>, string <code>+</code>, <code>instanceof</code>, and strict <code>boolean</code> conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Operators</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arithmetic</td><td><code>+ - * / %</code></td><td>Integer <code>/</code> truncates toward zero; <code>%</code> takes the sign of the dividend</td></tr><tr><td>Unary</td><td><code>+ - ++ -- !</code>, <code>~</code></td><td>Prefix <code>++i</code> increments then yields; postfix <code>i++</code> yields then increments</td></tr><tr><td>Relational</td><td><code>== != &lt; &lt;= &gt; &gt;=</code></td><td><code>==</code> on objects compares references</td></tr><tr><td>Logical</td><td><code>&amp;&amp; || !</code></td><td>Short-circuit; operands must be <code>boolean</code></td></tr><tr><td>Bitwise</td><td><code>&amp; | ^ ~</code></td><td>On <code>boolean</code> operands <code>&amp;</code>/<code>|</code> evaluate <em>both</em> sides</td></tr><tr><td>Shift</td><td><code>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td><td><code>&gt;&gt;</code> keeps the sign, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> fills with zeros</td></tr><tr><td>Assignment</td><td><code>= += -= *= /= %= &amp;= |= ^= &lt;&lt;= &gt;&gt;= &gt;&gt;&gt;=</code></td><td>Compound forms cast implicitly</td></tr><tr><td>Ternary</td><td><code>cond ? a : b</code></td><td>An expression — yields a value</td></tr><tr><td>Type</td><td><code>instanceof</code></td><td><code>obj instanceof String s</code> binds a variable (Java 16+)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math\"></i>Arithmetic details</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(7 / 2 + \" \" + -7 / 2 + \" \" + 7 % 3 + \" \" + -7 % 3);\n        System.out.println(Math.floorDiv(-7, 2) + \" \" + Math.floorMod(-7, 3));\n        System.out.println(5.5 % 2);\n        int i = 5;\n        int a = i++;          // a = 5, then i = 6\n        int b = ++i;          // i = 7, then b = 7\n        System.out.println(a + \" \" + b + \" \" + i);\n        String s = \"Sum: \" + 1 + 2;        // left to right: string concat\n        String t = 1 + 2 + \" is the sum\";  // 3 first, then concat\n        System.out.println(s + \" | \" + t);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>3 -3 1 -1\n-4 2\n1.5\n5 7 7\nSum: 12 | 3 is the sum</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>i = i++;</code> leaves <code>i</code> unchanged: the old value is saved, <code>i</code> is incremented, then the saved old value is assigned back. A classic trick question — never write it.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Short-circuit evaluation</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static boolean check(String label, boolean value) {\n        System.out.println(\"evaluating \" + label);\n        return value;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String s = null;\n        if (s != null &amp;&amp; s.length() &gt; 3) System.out.println(\"long\");   // safe: right side skipped\n        System.out.println(check(\"A\", false) &amp;&amp; check(\"B\", true));\n        System.out.println(check(\"C\", false) &amp; check(\"D\", true));       // non-short-circuit\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>evaluating A\nfalse\nevaluating C\nevaluating D\nfalse</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Bitwise &amp; shift operators</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int a = 0b1100, b = 0b1010;\n        System.out.println((a &amp; b) + \" \" + (a | b) + \" \" + (a ^ b) + \" \" + ~a);\n        System.out.println((1 &lt;&lt; 10) + \" \" + (-16 &gt;&gt; 2) + \" \" + (-16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 28));\n        int n = 12;\n        System.out.println(\"even? \" + ((n &amp; 1) == 0));\n        System.out.println(\"power of two? \" + (n &gt; 0 &amp;&amp; (n &amp; (n - 1)) == 0));\n        System.out.println(\"set bits: \" + Integer.bitCount(n));\n        int x = 3, y = 9;\n        x ^= y; y ^= x; x ^= y;                  // XOR swap\n        System.out.println(x + \" \" + y);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>8 14 6 -13\n1024 -4 15\neven? true\npower of two? false\nset bits: 2\n9 3</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-descending\"></i>Precedence (high → low)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Operators</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Postfix</td><td><code>expr++ expr--</code></td></tr><tr><td>Unary</td><td><code>++expr --expr + - ~ !</code> and casts</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplicative</td><td><code>* / %</code></td></tr><tr><td>Additive</td><td><code>+ -</code></td></tr><tr><td>Shift</td><td><code>&lt;&lt; &gt;&gt; &gt;&gt;&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td>Relational</td><td><code>&lt; &gt; &lt;= &gt;= instanceof</code></td></tr><tr><td>Equality</td><td><code>== !=</code></td></tr><tr><td>Bitwise</td><td><code>&amp;</code> then <code>^</code> then <code>|</code></td></tr><tr><td>Logical</td><td><code>&amp;&amp;</code> then <code>||</code></td></tr><tr><td>Ternary</td><td><code>? :</code></td></tr><tr><td>Assignment</td><td><code>= += -= …</code> (right-associative)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(2 + 3 * 4);\n        System.out.println((2 + 3) * 4);\n        System.out.println(1 + 2 &lt;&lt; 1);           // + binds tighter than &lt;&lt;\n        System.out.println(true || false &amp;&amp; false);   // &amp;&amp; before ||\n        int score = 72;\n        String grade = score &gt;= 90 ? \"A\" : score &gt;= 70 ? \"B\" : \"C\";\n        System.out.println(grade);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>14\n20\n6\ntrue\nB</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Tip</div><p>When in doubt, add parentheses. Readers shouldn't need the precedence table.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between &amp; and &amp;&amp;?</summary><div><p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> is logical AND with short-circuiting — the right side isn't evaluated if the left is <code>false</code>. <code>&amp;</code> on booleans evaluates both sides; on integers it is bitwise AND.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is &gt;&gt;&gt;?</summary><div><p>The unsigned right shift: it shifts in zeros from the left, regardless of sign. <code>&gt;&gt;</code> is the arithmetic shift, which copies the sign bit. For negative numbers they differ: <code>-16 &gt;&gt; 2</code> is <code>-4</code>, <code>-16 &gt;&gt;&gt; 28</code> is <code>15</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does System.out.println(1 + 2 + \"3\" + 4 + 5) print?</summary><div><p><code>3345</code>. Evaluation is left to right: <code>1 + 2</code> = <code>3</code>, then <code>3 + \"3\"</code> = <code>\"33\"</code>, and from then on every <code>+</code> is string concatenation.</p></div></details></section>"}